avfoundation: render subtitles through the shared osd_ahead pipeline

On Apple TV a subtitle frame that took longer than the queue lead to render
made the video late with it: flip_page rendered the frame's subtitles on the
VO thread and enqueued the video sample only afterwards. A heavy sign (the
Ex2 sample, 105-122 ms on an Apple TV 4K) cost a late video frame every
time it appeared.

Render them on the shared render-ahead pipeline (osd_ahead.[ch], as
vo_mediacodec does): draw_frame files the frame's request as soon as the
frame is known, the pipeline pre-renders the frame its cadence predicts
next and, while the OSD is static, the next subtitle event on a separate
renderer (0013), and flip_page presents the result. flip_page waits for it
only as avf_osd_wait_until allows: a timed frame keeps half its queue lead
for the video, and does not wait at all while the render thread is still
on an older frame; subtitles that arrive later are shown with a later
frame, and of several ready results only the newest is drawn. The composite
modes wait as the synchronous path did. When the render thread cannot
start, the synchronous path renders as before.

While paused no later frame comes. A frame shown while paused (a seek) has
no queue lead to protect and waits like a redraw, and a result that still
arrives after its frame wakes the VO thread, which redraws the frame on
screen with it without filing a new request.

The inline overlay now draws every item of a frame's list into one buffer;
before, a second item (the OSD, a secondary subtitle) replaced the first.

